.Dd August 17, 2026
.Dt INIT 8
.Os Sowa
.Sh NAME
.Nm init
.Nd process control initialization
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Ar runlevel
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is process 1: the kernel starts it once the root filesystem is mounted, and it
is the ancestor of everything else on the machine.
It reads
.Xr inittab 5 ,
runs the entries the current runlevel asks for, keeps the respawning ones
alive, reaps whatever the system orphans onto it, listens on
.Pa /run/initctl
for
.Xr telinit 8
and
.Xr shutdown 8 ,
and takes the machine down itself when it reaches runlevel 0 or 6.
.Pp
This is System V style init.
Nothing here supervises a service that dies: a daemon that exits stays exited
until something starts it again.
What is respawned is what
.Xr inittab 5
says to respawn, which in a shipped image is the login prompt on each console.
.Ss Runlevels
A runlevel is a state of the machine, named by one character, and the entries
in
.Xr inittab 5
say which of them each entry belongs to.
Sowa uses them as follows:
.Bl -tag -width "S  " -compact -offset indent
.It 0
halt or power off
.It 1
single user with the rc scripts run: services stopped, console login
.It 2
multi user
.It 3
multi user, and the default
.It 4
multi user, free for local use
.It 5
multi user, free for local use; nothing here manages a display
.It 6
reboot
.It S
single user without the rc scripts: a root shell and nothing else
.El
.Pp
The runlevel to enter at boot comes from the
.Cm initdefault
entry in
.Xr inittab 5 ,
unless the kernel command line overrides it.
Runlevels 2 through 5 differ only in what has been linked into their
.Pa /etc/rc.d/rc Ns Ar N Ns Pa .d
directory; see
.Xr chkconfig 8 .
.Ss The boot
.Nm
runs the
.Cm sysinit
entry first and waits for it.
In a shipped image that is
.Pa /etc/rc.d/rc.sysinit ,
which mounts
.Pa /proc ,
.Pa /sys ,
.Pa /dev
and
.Pa /run ,
sets the hostname and brings the loopback interface up.
Only then is a runlevel entered, which runs
.Pa /etc/rc.d/rc
against the links in that runlevel's directory.
.Pp
Each process
.Nm
starts is given the environment a service is expected to boot in and nothing
else:
.Ev PATH
is
.Pa /bin:/sbin:/usr/bin:/usr/sbin ,
.Ev HOME
is
.Pa / ,
.Ev CONSOLE
is
.Pa /dev/console ,
.Ev RUNLEVEL
and
.Ev PREVLEVEL
name the runlevel being entered and the one before it, and
.Ev INIT_VERSION
says which init this is.
A command containing a shell metacharacter is run through
.Pa /bin/sh -c ;
anything else is executed directly.
Its standard input, output and error are the console, unless the entry is
marked
.Qq @
in
.Xr inittab 5 ,
which is what a getty needs.
.Pp
A respawning entry that starts more than ten times in two minutes is treated as
broken: it is disabled for five minutes, with a line on the console saying so,
and then tried again.
.Ss The kernel command line
Arguments the kernel did not recognise are passed to
.Nm ,
which is how these have reached init since the beginning:
.Bl -tag -width "emergency" -offset indent
.It Ar 0-6 , Cm S , Cm s
enter that runlevel instead of the
.Cm initdefault
one.
.It Cm single , Fl s
runlevel S: everything mounted, nothing started, a root shell on the console.
.It Cm emergency , Fl b
a root shell and nothing else, without reading
.Xr inittab 5
at all.
This is what to boot when the inittab itself is the problem.
.El
.Ss Shutdown
Reaching runlevel 0 or 6 is what brings the machine down, and
.Nm
performs the last phase itself rather than leaving it to a script.
It runs the runlevel's own K links first, so the services stop in order, then
signals every remaining process with
.Dv SIGTERM ,
waits, sends
.Dv SIGKILL ,
flushes the filesystems and remounts them read-only, and makes the reboot,
halt or power-off call.
.Pp
Whether runlevel 0 halts or powers off is carried in the request:
.Xr halt 8
asks for one and
.Xr poweroff 8
for the other.
A request with nothing to say powers the machine off, which is what a machine
with no operator standing at it wants.
.Ss Signals
.Bl -tag -width SIGWINCH
.It Dv SIGHUP
reread
.Xr inittab 5
without changing the runlevel.
.Xr telinit 8 Cm q
sends this.
.It Dv SIGUSR1
close the control FIFO and create it again.
.Pa /run
is a tmpfs mounted by
.Pa rc.sysinit
after
.Nm
has already made its FIFO, so without this the FIFO would be under the mount
and nothing could reach it.
.It Dv SIGINT
run the
.Cm ctrlaltdel
entry.
The kernel sends this when Ctrl-Alt-Del is pressed.
.It Dv SIGPWR
run the
.Cm powerfail
or
.Cm powerwait
entries.
A UPS daemon sends this; nothing in the base image does.
.It Dv SIGWINCH
run the
.Cm kbrequest
entry, which the kernel asks for through the KeyboardSignal keymap action.
.It Dv SIGTERM
ignored, with a line on the console.
Use
.Xr telinit 8
or
.Xr shutdown 8 .
.El
.Ss Run as anything but process 1
.Nm
run by a user forwards a runlevel request to the real one and exits, exactly as
System V's did, so
.Ic init 1
and
.Ic telinit 1
are the same instruction.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "/run/initrunlevel" -compact
.It Pa /etc/inittab
what to run, and when.
.It Pa /run/initctl
the FIFO requests arrive on.
.Pa /dev/initctl
is a symbolic link to it.
.It Pa /run/initrunlevel
the previous and current runlevel, as
.Xr runlevel 8
reports them.
System V read this out of utmp; glibc 2.42 removed the functions that wrote
utmp, so it is kept here instead.
.It Pa /etc/rc.d/rc.sysinit
the
.Cm sysinit
entry in the shipped inittab.
.It Pa /etc/rc.d/rc
the runlevel driver, run for every runlevel.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr inittab 5 ,
.Xr chkconfig 8 ,
.Xr halt 8 ,
.Xr runlevel 8 ,
.Xr service 8 ,
.Xr shutdown 8 ,
.Xr telinit 8
.Sh HISTORY
.Nm
is
.Nm sowa-init ,
written specifically for Sowa.
It speaks the System V request protocol on
.Pa /run/initctl ,
so anything that already knows how to drive that FIFO can drive this.
